   "subject": "Re: ploidy.mean.cn",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Jessie,<div><br></div><div>The <a href=\"http://ploidy.mean.cn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://ploidy.mean.cn&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792481707865000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1YvzG_CElL92X6jnR7uJ_1\">ploidy.mean.cn</a> is the weighted mean of the segments copy number.</div><div>In theory, this and the ploidy.estimate should be very close, but as you can see, it&#39;s not always the case.</div><div><br></div><div>Looking at your results, there is a wide confidence interval around the ploidy estimate, if this is WES there might be biases effecting the depth ratio profile, there are many factors that could result in such difference.</div><div>As a rule of thumb, when there are discrepancies in the two values, the <a href=\"http://ploidy.mean.cn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://ploidy.mean.cn&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792481707865000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1YvzG_CElL92X6jnR7uJ_1\">ploidy.mean.cn</a> is more correct, since it represent the segments and not an estimate of the model.</div><div><br></div><div>Best</div><div><br></div><div>Francesco</div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
